#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd806d5-cac7-5ab8-a47e-d04d92de858c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262912} {:answer/id #uuid "3d044d43-04b6-5470-b760-526c2119b7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913} {:answer/id #uuid "feab9798-b8b0-5866-8c2e-cfeb09826f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262914} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2a0c28-1458-5844-8c84-fc64358082b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262915} {:answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916} :answer-field/id #uuid "0036bd73-610c-537a-a14e-f81117e7e6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ced084c-e5cc-510a-ae57-0a31b5977172" :question/math-academy-id "q-112877" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254323} {:answer/id #uuid "8b882184-5e97-5056-9ec0-a1434002a13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254324} {:answer/id #uuid "10d7958e-1868-5d93-8a9d-23272d7e1433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254325} {:answer/id #uuid "fa815d57-dd6d-544f-bee3-ecf188611a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254326} {:answer/id #uuid "5b605b48-1f56-5b52-a0a4-da41b6e648f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254323} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be6948b-0bbe-50e4-8e11-a063db8462a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6369285e-2dff-5b5c-a6d1-64b6011c7055" :question/math-academy-id "q-112960" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db7807c-da93-5f6f-8bf5-832fec41bb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3db7807c-da93-5f6f-8bf5-832fec41bb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327785} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f0f861b-9518-5001-9a35-06d48a9f94df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f82cc12-d956-5296-9d23-6bdadb185052" :question/math-academy-id "q-323218" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d653916d-5138-5616-a1d4-14509ffa1ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d653916d-5138-5616-a1d4-14509ffa1ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327788} :answer-field/id #uuid "d03e1aea-f397-5196-9173-82319cdcecfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d4146ea-ef74-54fb-b8a7-8f7978b7e5e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d4146ea-ef74-54fb-b8a7-8f7978b7e5e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327790} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b61613f-414a-5f86-b6d2-5d1181143f46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ce3642-5223-5ab2-bc23-63df075b970e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09ce3642-5223-5ab2-bc23-63df075b970e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327792} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bd080ed-486a-56f7-a976-1b1e5c9c2610" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9930c420-f372-5a27-8b7c-8f38c6bfb53a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323819" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d07b4d3a-0a17-5f2c-a16c-b0b451b8dc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327795} {:answer/id #uuid "43ce1fdf-8e8a-54cb-b634-270a6857697a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327796} {:answer/id #uuid "888c2c61-5acb-5360-8c19-a73b3edefb26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327797} {:answer/id #uuid "70087bf9-e860-570f-9dde-409a5651d33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327798} {:answer/id #uuid "b63728da-f7ac-58fb-9909-cfdfa6096ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07b4d3a-0a17-5f2c-a16c-b0b451b8dc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327795} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f7a183-39dd-5980-9f62-bb7338d90592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95a80834-eafd-54c7-aa21-4bf9e18db155" :question/math-academy-id "q-38293" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6$ has one of its roots in the interval $(0,3),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,3),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=1,-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n1-2+3=2.\n$$"}]}
